@@ -1,36 +1,40 @@
 {
-  "id": "grammar.b2.30.01.marcadores-contraste-adversativo",
-  "title": "Adversative Contrast Discourse Markers",
+  "id": "grammar.b2.30.01.contrast-markers",
+  "title": "Contrast Markers",
   "sections": [
     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "Los marcadores de contraste adversativo introducen una objeción o limitación sustantiva respecto al enunciado anterior, sin llegar a anular su validez fundamental. En el nivel B2 formal, señalan la dialéctica entre premisas contrapuestas."
+      "content": "You know *pero* from A1, *sin embargo* from A2 Unit 28, *no obstante* from B1 Unit 1 and *en cambio* from an A2 Unit 28 word list. All of them set one idea against another. This lesson shows five markers that do different jobs, and how they differ from *pero*.\n\n*Pero* joins two clauses inside one sentence, and no comma follows it. The markers below usually start a new clause, after a full stop or a semicolon, and a comma follows them.\n\n• *en cambio*: two things are compared and found different (\"whereas\", \"on the other hand\"). It can also sit in the middle of the second clause, between commas.\n• *por el contrario*: the opposite of what was just said, or just expected, is true. It often follows a negative.\n• *al contrario*: the same idea. It is also the natural one-word answer in conversation.\n• *no obstante*: \"nevertheless\", a more formal *sin embargo*. It admits a fact and goes on in spite of it.\n• *ahora bien*: \"that said\", \"now then\". It does not say the opposite. It adds a point that limits what you have just accepted."
     },
     {
       "type": "table",
-      "title": "Marcadores adversativos en el ensayo culto",
+      "title": "Five markers, five jobs",
       "rows": [
         [
-          "Las expediciones contaban con artillería pesada; sin embargo, no lograron quebrar el cerco.",
-          "The expeditions had heavy artillery; however, they failed to break the siege."
+          "Mi hermana vive en la ciudad; yo, en cambio, vivo en el campo.",
+          "My sister lives in the city; I, on the other hand, live in the country."
         ],
         [
-          "El terreno era árido y hostil; no obstante, los sertanejos resistieron con firmeza sobrehumana.",
-          "The terrain was arid and hostile; nevertheless, the sertanejos resisted with superhuman firmness."
+          "No es caro; por el contrario, es muy barato.",
+          "It is not expensive; on the contrary, it is very cheap."
         ],
         [
-          "Los rebeldes carecían de municiones modernas; con todo, rechazaron cuatro asaltos consecutivos.",
-          "The rebels lacked modern ammunition; even so, they repelled four consecutive assaults."
+          "—¿Te molesta el ruido? —Al contrario, me gusta.",
+          "\"Does the noise bother you?\" \"Quite the opposite, I like it.\""
         ],
         [
-          "El ejército sufrió bajas cuantiosas; aun así, el general ordenó mantener la posición.",
-          "The army suffered heavy casualties; even so, the general ordered the position maintained."
+          "Tiene poca experiencia; no obstante, hace un buen trabajo.",
+          "He has little experience; nevertheless, he does a good job."
+        ],
+        [
+          "El plan es bueno. Ahora bien, cuesta mucho dinero.",
+          "The plan is good. That said, it costs a lot of money."
         ]
       ]
     },
     {
       "type": "tip",
-      "content": "'Sin embargo' y 'no obstante' exigen aislamiento ortográfico mediante comas, o bien punto y coma anterior y coma posterior cuando coordinan dos oraciones independientes."
+      "content": "*Pero* is the everyday word: *Es bueno, pero caro.* Use these markers when you want to say what kind of contrast you mean: a comparison (*en cambio*), the opposite (*por el contrario*, *al contrario*), a fact that does not stop you (*no obstante*) or a limit (*ahora bien*)."
     }
   ]
 }
diff --git a/content/es-latam/grammar/b2/b2-30-02-a-gr.json b/content/es-latam/grammar/b2/b2-30-02-a-gr.json
