-  "title": "Attenuated Opposition & Restrictive Markers",
+  "id": "grammar.b2.30.05.eso-si-de-todos-modos",
+  "title": "Softening an Objection",
   "sections": [
     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "Los marcadores de oposición atenuada suavizan la fricción entre enunciados contrarios, subrayando la perseverancia del sujeto a pesar de los obstáculos objetivos. Son habituales en la prosa historiográfica épica."
+      "content": "You know *ahora bien* (\"that said\") from the first lesson of this unit. Here it joins two phrases that soften an opposition instead of cancelling what came before:\n\n• *eso sí*: \"mind you\", \"but only if\". You accept something and add a condition or a limit. It is conversational, and *sí* keeps its accent.\n• *de todos modos* (also *de todas formas*): \"anyway\", \"in any case\". What you say or do stays the same whatever happens.\n• *ahora bien*: more formal than *eso sí*. It raises a point that limits what you accepted.\n\nAll three keep the first idea true. What differs is what they add: *eso sí* adds a condition, *de todos modos* says that nothing changes, and *ahora bien* adds a limit."
     },
     {
       "type": "table",
-      "title": "Oposición atenuada y resiliencia",
